{
  "data": {
    "data": {
      "object": {
        "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue/76266",
        "type": "JiraWorkItem",
        "key": "NETE-737",
        "summary": "GlobalConnect TLS Handshake Failure",
        "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737"
      },
      "relationships": [
        {
          "relationshipName": "jira_work_item_has_jira_work_item_comment",
          "targetObjectType": "JiraWorkItemComment",
          "targets": [
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48035",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "Alex and I made an initial investigation. The device’s APN is not configured properly, and the device is not connecting to the cellular network.\nWe see other traffic on that CIDR and there is no issue with the routing as far as we know, so the tunnel itself appears healthy.\nThe log files they have provided are dated late June 2026, indicating this has been a problem for a while, but they aren’t reporting any other device issues.\nWe have run into an oddity. Both DMG and GlobalConnect share 10.81.192.0/19 CIDRs as part of their traffic selectors. That seems really odd.",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48035"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48036",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "andy.cutright@OCIS20251000 net-eng % claude --name global-connect-tls-issue",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48036"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48042",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "I checked — it's a look-alike, not an actual overlap. DMG and us_connect both have a 100.81.x.0/19 and a 10.242.x.0/19, which is why it\n  jumped out, but they're different /19 blocks:\n  ┌────────────┬───────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────────────┐\n  │    Conn    │     Peer      │ 100.81 selector │      Range      │ Contains 100.81.201.96? │\n  ├────────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤\n  │ us_connect │ 18.116.7.62   │ 100.81.192.0/19 │ .192.0–.223.255 │ ✅ yes                  │\n  ├────────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤\n  │ DMG        │ 20.121.98.201 │ 100.81.128.0/19 │ .128.0–.159.255 │ ❌ no                   │\n  ├────────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤\n  │ novacharge │ (other)       │ 100.81.161.0/24 │ .161.0–.161.255 │ ❌ no                   │\n  └────────────┴───────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘\n  /19 steps in 32-address-of-the-third-octet blocks, so .128/19 (128–159) and .192/19 (192–223) don't touch. Same story on the 10.242 side:\n  DMG is .128/19, us_connect is .160/19 — adjacent, not overlapping. The device at .201 lives only in us_connect's block.\n  And the live kernel already proves there's no misdirection: the XFRM policy you pulled for 100.81.192.0/19 templates to 18.116.7.62\n  (us_connect's peer) — if DMG were stealing that selector it'd template to 20.121.98.201. It doesn't.\n  So the overlap theory is ruled out, and it reinforces where we landed: the tunnel is healthy and correctly owned — the dark device is\n  downstream (device offline on carrier, most likely, or the DIA/CSR return path). Good instinct to check it, though; overlapping selectors\n  are a real failure mode here, this just isn't a case of it.",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48042"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48043",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "Alex has emailed the customer, waiting for response",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48043"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48088",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "  995  claude --name global-connect-tls-issue in net-eng repo",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48088"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48089",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "  Current state — Global Connect \"Transactions failing in Canada\" (PWS SIM)\n  Verdict so far: not an IPSec/tunnel fault on our side. The us_connect Libreswan tunnel (box ipsec-all, peer 18.116.7.62) is healthy — XFRM\n  policies (out/in/fwd) and SA pairs are established for the device selector 100.81.192.0/19 ↔ 172.16.13.0/24, and the tunnel shows active\n  bidirectional traffic for the full CIDR. A live trace saw other devices in the range working (e.g. 100.81.201.86 doing DNS/HTTP to\n  172.16.13.x), but no traffic from the ticket device.\n  Device/SIM side is the open question:\n* OptConnect SIM = CCID 8901990000004516095 (APN lattigopriv, IP 100.81.194.29); comparison SIM 89852350225220062407 is Granite\n  (non-OptConnect) and is the \"works\" case.\n* Carrier records: our SIM was inactive 6/30→7/16, then ~3.78 MB on 7/16 that does line up with the app's error window.\n* But the 7/16 terminal log shows the device on wired Ethernet all day, never cellular — ICCID/APN/IP don't appear in it. Ethernet log and\n  carrier usage disagree.\n* IP/SIM identity is drifting across messages (100.81.201.96 vs 100.81.194.29 vs 100.81.201.86) — needs reconciling.\n  Next step: force the terminal onto the PWS SIM and capture on both ends during a failed transaction; request the matching cellular-side\n  device log (Alex has asked Simform for this).",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48089"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48125",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "Root cause identified (net-eng) — path-MTU black-hole on the TEAL CSR tunnel return leg\nSummary: Transaction failures on the PWS (lattigopriv/Teal) SIM are caused by a path-MTU black-hole on our TEAL CSR IPSec tunnel (Tunnel0), in the device-return direction. The customer's private/backend traffic is unaffected — only large TLS payloads (the Apriva payment handshake) fail. This is on our side and has a precedented fix.\nFailing endpoint: ws.accounts.apriva.com (51.143.124.200:443) — the Apriva OAuth token call, the first step of a transaction, so nothing downstream (paymentwebservice.apriva.com charge/capture) is ever reached.\nEvidence\n* Device logs (PWS_Test.zip, terminal 0000562242038939, 2026-07-16 UTC): on lattigopriv (Bell, 100.81.194.29) the device hits ws.accounts.apriva.com:443 — TCP connects, TLS ClientHello sent, ServerHello never returns, ~30 s timeout (curl 28), six times. Same probe succeeded earlier on the Granite/wbdata SIM. The private routing check to the customer DC (172.16.13.232) succeeds on the PWS SIM → the tunnel/private path is healthy.\n* Live TEAL CSR (CSR19-PRI-TEAL): forward path to Apriva egresses Gi2 (default route → AWS VPC); return path to the device (100.81.0.0/16) is over Tunnel0 (BGP from Teal DC AS 399456, nh 10.100.90.2). The path is asymmetric.\n* show interfaces: Gi2 MTU 1500; Tunnel0 transport MTU 1422 (~78 B ESP overhead). Tunnel0 has no ip tcp adjust-mss and no crypto ipsec df-bit clear.\n* Mechanism: with no MSS clamp the session negotiates ~1460 MSS; the large ServerHello+Certificate (~1500 B) exceeds the 1422 tunnel MTU on the return leg, DF is set, so it is dropped and PMTUD black-holes. Small packets (handshake, ClientHello, all private/backend traffic) pass — matching the log exactly.\nRuled out\n* Not a TLS/certificate issue — SSL_VERIFYPEER=0; the \"error setting certificate verify locations\" line is benign/pre-handshake; the failure is a timeout, not a cert rejection; no ServerHello ever arrives.\n* Not \"expected on a private tunnel\" — TCP connects to Apriva, so public egress exists; it is degraded, not absent.\n* Not the ipsec-all Libreswan box — a TLS capture there shows only VZW-PN/VDF private traffic to the customer DC; no 100.81.x / no Apriva. The Apriva flow does not transit ipsec-all.\nStep 1 — confirm (CSR Embedded Packet Capture, read-only / non-disruptive)\nOn the active TEAL CSR, during a customer repro (force terminal onto PWS SIM + run a transaction):\nip access-list extended TLSCHK\n permit tcp host 100.81.194.29 host 51.143.124.200\n permit tcp host 51.143.124.200 host 100.81.194.29\nmonitor capture CAP access-list TLSCHK buffer size 10\nmonitor capture CAP interface GigabitEthernet2 both\nmonitor capture CAP interface Tunnel0 both\nmonitor capture CAP start\n!  ... repro ...\nmonitor capture CAP stop\nshow monitor capture CAP buffer brief\nmonitor capture CAP export tftp://172.31.150.20/teal-tls.pcap\nPull /srv/tftp/teal-tls.pcap from the jump host; in Wireshark filter tls.handshake.type == 2 (ServerHello). Expected: ServerHello arrives inbound on Gi2 and does not egress Tunnel0 — proving both that the flow transits this box and that the tunnel is the drop point. Also verify this is the active box and that the VPC route table returns 100.81.0.0/16 to this CSR's ENI.\nStep 2 — fix (only after Step 1 confirms)\nAdd ip tcp adjust-mss 1360 on TEAL Tunnel0 (optionally crypto ipsec df-bit clear), matching the existing TMO-DIRECT/ATT tunnels that already carry this clamp. 1360 is safely under the 1422 transport MTU (max MSS ~1382). Apply as a reviewed change-set, not a live hand-edit. Teal is support-only / under litigation → needs the appropriate approvals.\nNext step: net-eng to schedule the capture + change-set. No customer/device change required.",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48125"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48347",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "Update — 2026-07-29 UAT demo capture reproduces the failure; requesting a device-side traceroute to confirm the path\nReviewed a fresh device log (PWS_demo_logs.txt), same terminal 000562242038939, same path as before — GSM IP 100.81.194.29 (lattigopriv/Bell), covering 2026-07-29 00:17–14:05 UTC. It reproduces the exact split we diagnosed:\n* Public (Apriva) fails: 4/4 healthchecks to https://paymentwebservice.apriva.com/healthcheck timed out — curl code 28 at the 15s limit, \"Internet is not available.\" Zero successes.\n* Private (tunnel) healthy: REST calls to device-external-test-api.globconnect.priv returned http 200 throughout.\nSame PMTU black-hole signature (TCP/TLS opens, the large ServerHello+Certificate never returns → 15s timeout). No change from the 2026-07-16 capture — the MTU fix on the TEAL CSR Tunnel0 has not been applied / taken effect.\nNew detail: the device app clamps MSS = 1356, but only on its private REST calls (Restclient.cpp); the public Apriva check runs a different path (GCTConnectionManager::CheckPublicRouting) with no MSS clamp. So there is no app-side mitigation on the failing path — the fix still has to be the CSR-side ip tcp adjust-mss on Tunnel0.\nUnrelated noise: a transient SIM-not-ready → reboot around 12:02–12:04 (curl code 6, couldn't-resolve). Modem blip, not the MTU issue.\nStill unconfirmed — that the Apriva traffic actually transits this CSR. The path is inferred from the CSR forwarding tables (CEF), not from an observed flow. Open: active-vs-standby CSR, whether the AWS VPC returns 100.81/16 to this CSR's ENI, and no capture of the Apriva 5-tuple yet.\nAsk: in parallel with the on-box EPC capture, we've requested a traceroute from the device to Apriva (51.143.124.200 / paymentwebservice.apriva.com) via the support team. If the internal hops show the Teal DC → our CSR gateway, that's direct confirmation the path runs through our router — cheapest way to close the last assumption before we schedule the change-set.",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48347"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48348",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "For support — suggested reply to the customer (relaying the traceroute request)\n> Hi [name],Thanks for the latest logs — they've been helpful. Our Network Engineering team has confirmed the pattern: the terminal reaches everything on the private/internal side just fine, but its secure connection to the Apriva payment gateway is timing out.To pinpoint exactly where along the path that connection is breaking down, Network Engineering would like a traceroute from the affected terminal out to Apriva. If you can get to the device's command line (the same way the previous logs were pulled), please run these and send back the full output:nslookup paymentwebservice.apriva.com\ntraceroute paymentwebservice.apriva.com\ntraceroute 51.143.124.200If traceroute isn't available on the device, tracepath with the same targets works too. Please run them while the device is on the SIM that's failing (the one showing IP 100.81.194.29), so we're tracing the right path.This will show us the exact hops the payment traffic takes and where it stops, which is the last piece we need before applying the network-side fix. Appreciate the help.\nReviewer note — what to look for in the returned traceroute (internal):\n* First internal hops matter most. If the early hops show the Teal DC → our CSR gateway, that confirms the Apriva traffic transits our router and locks in the diagnosis. If it does not route through us, that redirects the investigation before we touch the CSR.\n* Both a hostname and IP target are requested on purpose: the hostname trace also confirms DNS resolves; the 51.143.124.200 trace runs even if DNS is flaky. Both are the Apriva public side.\n* Pair this with the on-box EPC capture already described above — traceroute proves the path, EPC proves the black-hole (ServerHello arrives on Gi2, never egresses Tunnel0).\nAttachment: PWS_demo_logs.txt (2026-07-29 UAT demo capture) attached to this issue for reference.",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48348"
            },
            {
              "ari": "ari:cloud:jira:f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094:issue-comment/76266/48349",
              "type": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "__typename": "JiraWorkItemComment",
              "content": "📎 1 File\n",
              "webUrl": "https://optconnect.atlassian.net/browse/NETE-737?focusedCommentId=48349"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "pagination": {
        "hasNextPage": true,
        "endCursor": "IAo="
      }
