{
  "expand": "renderedFields,names,schema,operations,editmeta,changelog,versionedRepresentations",
  "id": "75897",
  "self": "https://api.atlassian.com/ex/jira/f13b80b2-7d19-42ca-9382-15dbd28e1094/rest/api/3/issue/75897",
  "key": "NETE-722",
  "fields": {
    "summary": "NovaCharge Overage Analysis",
    "customfield_10020": [
      {
        "id": 4342,
        "name": "NE 26 Sprint 9",
        "state": "closed",
        "boardId": 248,
        "goal": "17 days | 15 sp planned | 15 sp total",
        "startDate": "2026-07-15T15:58:50.000Z",
        "endDate": "2026-08-14T23:00:00.000Z",
        "completeDate": "2026-08-17T12:37:34.950Z"
      },
      {
        "id": 4442,
        "name": "NE 26 Sprint 10",
        "state": "active",
        "boardId": 248,
        "goal": "13 days | 5 sp planned | 10 sp total",
        "startDate": "2026-08-17T12:37:59.093Z",
        "endDate": "2026-09-02T06:00:00.000Z"
      }
    ],
    "description": "```\nI'm following up on this incorrect billing situation and associated technical issue, again.  \n\nOn the technical issue: \nIt’s been nearly 9 weeks since we identified the incorrect billing that subsequently turned into a routing issue in the Optconnect/PWS network.  You have taken the action to identify the root cause and provide us with a proper technical response which should then lead to a proper billing outcome.  \n\nAs of now, we’ve not heard from you on the root cause of the technical issue, nor the corrections you have made the control the significant data overages that have been assigned to the SIMs in our account.\n```",
    "comment": {
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": "48153",
          "body": "andy.cutright@OCIS20251000 Machine_configuration % claude --name novacharge-nete-722",
          "created": "2026-07-21T13:48:18.442-0600",
          "updated": "2026-07-21T13:48:18.442-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "48154",
          "body": "h2. Root-cause findings — NovaCharge AT&T overage (2026-07-21)\n\n_Evidence:_ device logs from NovaCharge (Jonathan Gilbert, unit IC3-221700028, physical AT&T SIM, IMSI 310170… = AT&T Mobility) + decrypted CSR reference config (CSR05/CSR06 = live config).\n\nh3. 1. Router identification (naming trap)  \nThe billing-plan string _NovaCharge-ATT-PN-PPU_ does NOT map to the Cisco _ATT-PN_ carrier pair. These devices are handled by the _ATT_ pair _CSR05-PRI-ATT / CSR06-SEC-ATT_, not ATT-PN (CSR23/CSR24).\n\n* CSR05/06: {{ip route 192.168.150.0 255.255.255.0 GigabitEthernet2 172.31.4.1 name NovaCharge}}\n* CSR23/24: zero NovaCharge / 192.168.150.x references.\n\nh3. 2. Root cause — split tunnel, not fully private  \nChargers backhaul over the AT&T Jasper VTI (CSR05 Tunnel0 \"VTI_TUNNEL_JASPER_DC1\", dest 208.184.161.18, 10.100.40.1/30; APN 10.64.0.0/13). Routing on the CSR is split:\n\n* _Customer-DC route:_ 192.168.150.0/24 → 172.31.4.1 — private path to their OCPP DC; routed, not NAT'd. Matches device traceroute {{10.100.40.1 → 172.31.2.133 → 192.168.150.110}}.\n* _Default route:_ 0.0.0.0/0 → 172.31.4.1 — a _separate_ path; public-bound traffic (e.g. google.com) follows this toward shared PWS transit, NOT NovaCharge's tunnel and outside their visibility.\n* _NAT:_ Gi2 overload only for destination-allowlisted services (CalAmp, private DNS 172.31.0.2, specific hosts). No permit-any NAT on the CSR; arbitrary public egress is NAT'd upstream at the PWS DC edge.\n\nThis is working as the split-tunnel design intends — not tunnel leakage, a routing fault, or an APN misconfig. The gap is that AT&T is delivered as split-tunnel while the customer expected \"fully private.\" _VZW and T-Mobile are provisioned true-private (all traffic forced through the tunnel), which is why the bypass appears only on AT&T._\n\nh3. 3. Billing — mechanism only, NOT a proven cause  \nThis explains a _pathway_ by which traffic can leave the SIM outside the tunnel. It does NOT establish what drove the overages or how much. The logs Jonathan supplied are manual diagnostics only (traceroute / nslookup / interface state) — _no byte counters, flow records, or packet capture, i.e. no evidence of actual overage-driving traffic._ No billing conclusion should be drawn yet.\n\nTo tie root cause to the charges we need usage/volume data:\n\n* AT&T Control Center per-SIM usage by destination/day for the flagged ICCIDs over the disputed period (billing source of truth); and/or\n* NetFlow / scoped capture on CSR05/06 for the NovaCharge tunnel.\n* Confirm which SIMs are actually flagged (only one unit was tested, unknown if it's a high-overage SIM). The tested unit is a Wi-Fi gateway (≤20 downstream clients) — downstream devices are a candidate source.\n\nh3. 4. Remediation options\n\n# Move AT&T SIMs to a true-private APN (matching VZW/TMO) — forces all traffic through the tunnel, public becomes unreachable. _Recommended._ Carrier feasibility for moving existing SIMs = _Sales responsibility._\n\n# Keep split-tunnel, firewall egress at the device — burden on NovaCharge; weak for downstream Wi-Fi clients.\n\n# Status quo + explicit disclosure.\n\nh3. 5. Open items\n\n* _eSIM capture_ — complaint names AT&T + eSIM, but only physical-AT&T evidence provided. Request same traceroute/nslookup/nm_state from an eSIM unit (likely Teal PWS-GREY-…-AVT, also split-tunnel).\n* _Carrier feasibility_ for true-private APN move — Sales.\n* Reconcile the \"DIA Split Tunnel\" (§1) vs \"Fully Private\" (§2) labeling in the 2026-07-17 customer summary before it goes back out.\n\n",
          "created": "2026-07-21T14:26:14.314-0600",
          "updated": "2026-07-21T14:26:14.314-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "48155",
          "body": "Replied to Jonathon request for eSIM trace",
          "created": "2026-07-21T14:35:39.861-0600",
          "updated": "2026-07-21T14:35:39.861-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "48344",
          "body": "Follow-up email sent 2026-07-30 — replied on the \"OptConnect - NovaCharge - Follow up meeting\" thread (to Jonathan, cc Mark/Oscar/Marilyn/Rob/Lacey/net-eng), chasing the eSIM-unit trace (traceroute / nslookup / interface + network-manager state) needed to finish the eSIM-side root cause and tie it to usage before the billing/remediation discussion.",
          "created": "2026-07-30T09:38:40.249-0600",
          "updated": "2026-07-30T09:38:40.249-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "48563",
          "body": "**Analysis of NovaCharge-provided device log (**`opt-eSim-logs.txt`, IC80A charger `IC80A-EX-1193-1A19-1-2238-00090`)\n\n_SIM/network identity:_\n\n* Cellular WAN IP: `100.81.161.51` — confirmed by NovaCharge as well.\n* This falls inside `100.81.161.0/24`, which NovaCharge allocated for the Teal eSIM pool.\n* IMSI home network is MCC 204 (Netherlands), while the device is actually registered on MCC 302 / MNC 610 (Canada) — consistent with an eSIM/MVNO identity (Teal) roaming onto a Canadian carrier network for service.\n\n_Routing analysis (comparing the log's two traceroutes against our Teal IPsec tunnel config):_\n\n* `Tunnel0` config: `ip address 10.100.90.1 255.255.255.252`, `tunnel destination 191.96.97.214`.\n* Traceroute to `ocpp.chargeupev.com` (resolves to `192.168.150.110`) shows hop 4 = `10.100.90.1` — an exact match to our Tunnel0 interface address. This traffic is confirmed transiting our Teal IPsec tunnel into our AWS-side network (us-west-1), where that customer host lives.\n* Traceroute to `google.com` shows hop 4 = `191.96.97.213` — one IP off our tunnel peer (`191.96.97.214`), almost certainly Teal's own carrier-side gateway sitting next to our tunnel endpoint — then breaks out directly to the public internet. This traffic never enters our network.\n\n_Conclusion:_ Teal is not full-tunneling this eSIM to us. Only traffic to prefixes we've specifically routed through the IPsec tunnel (e.g. the customer's OCPP host subnet) transits our network — general/internet-bound traffic from the SIM egresses directly out Teal's own gateway, outside our visibility and control.\n\n_Implication for the overage/billing dispute:_ any data this SIM sends that isn't destined for our routed prefixes occurs entirely outside our network — we have no visibility into or control over that usage from our side. Next step is to confirm with Teal/NovaCharge whether this split-tunnel behavior is the intended configuration for this SIM plan, since it directly affects who can account for and control the data driving the overage charges.",
          "created": "2026-08-17T12:07:43.660-0600",
          "updated": "2026-08-17T12:07:43.660-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "48640",
          "body": "Asked Mark to get Oscar to answer the “deployed regions” question. ",
          "created": "2026-08-19T14:20:10.341-0600",
          "updated": "2026-08-19T14:20:10.341-0600",
          "author": {
            "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
            "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
