        "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
        "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "48344",
      "body": "Follow-up email sent 2026-07-30 — replied on the \"OptConnect - NovaCharge - Follow up meeting\" thread (to Jonathan, cc Mark/Oscar/Marilyn/Rob/Lacey/net-eng), chasing the eSIM-unit trace (traceroute / nslookup / interface + network-manager state) needed to finish the eSIM-side root cause and tie it to usage before the billing/remediation discussion.",
      "created": "2026-07-30T09:38:40.249-0600",
      "updated": "2026-07-30T09:38:40.249-0600",
      "author": {
        "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
        "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "48563",
      "body": "**Analysis of NovaCharge-provided device log (**`opt-eSim-logs.txt`, IC80A charger `IC80A-EX-1193-1A19-1-2238-00090`)\n\n_SIM/network identity:_\n\n* Cellular WAN IP: `100.81.161.51` — confirmed by NovaCharge as well.\n* This falls inside `100.81.161.0/24`, which NovaCharge allocated for the Teal eSIM pool.\n* IMSI home network is MCC 204 (Netherlands), while the device is actually registered on MCC 302 / MNC 610 (Canada) — consistent with an eSIM/MVNO identity (Teal) roaming onto a Canadian carrier network for service.\n\n_Routing analysis (comparing the log's two traceroutes against our Teal IPsec tunnel config):_\n\n* `Tunnel0` config: `ip address 10.100.90.1 255.255.255.252`, `tunnel destination 191.96.97.214`.\n* Traceroute to `ocpp.chargeupev.com` (resolves to `192.168.150.110`) shows hop 4 = `10.100.90.1` — an exact match to our Tunnel0 interface address. This traffic is confirmed transiting our Teal IPsec tunnel into our AWS-side network (us-west-1), where that customer host lives.\n* Traceroute to `google.com` shows hop 4 = `191.96.97.213` — one IP off our tunnel peer (`191.96.97.214`), almost certainly Teal's own carrier-side gateway sitting next to our tunnel endpoint — then breaks out directly to the public internet. This traffic never enters our network.\n\n_Conclusion:_ Teal is not full-tunneling this eSIM to us. Only traffic to prefixes we've specifically routed through the IPsec tunnel (e.g. the customer's OCPP host subnet) transits our network — general/internet-bound traffic from the SIM egresses directly out Teal's own gateway, outside our visibility and control.\n\n_Implication for the overage/billing dispute:_ any data this SIM sends that isn't destined for our routed prefixes occurs entirely outside our network — we have no visibility into or control over that usage from our side. Next step is to confirm with Teal/NovaCharge whether this split-tunnel behavior is the intended configuration for this SIM plan, since it directly affects who can account for and control the data driving the overage charges.",
      "created": "2026-08-17T12:07:43.660-0600",
      "updated": "2026-08-17T12:07:43.660-0600",
      "author": {
        "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
        "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "48640",
      "body": "Asked Mark to get Oscar to answer the “deployed regions” question. ",
      "created": "2026-08-19T14:20:10.341-0600",
      "updated": "2026-08-19T14:20:10.341-0600",
      "author": {
        "accountId": "63fe47d2f00d095406f26040",
        "displayName": "Andy Cutright"
      }
    }
  ]
}

